  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe combination of clear skies, light winds, and low dew points\nwill allow for another night of radiational cooling tonight.\nForecast lows are mostly based on MOS guidance, with lower to\nmiddle 40s expected across the northern valleys and lower to\nmiddle 50s elsewhere. Valley fog is once again likely,\nespecially towards dawn as temperatures cool to near their dew\npoint and a large temperature gradient between air and water\nsets up.\n\nSurface high pressure will drift towards the Canadian Maritimes\non Saturday, allowing for light southeasterly flow across the\nregion. Other than some diurnally driven cu across the foothills\nand points north, mainly sunny skies will prevail with high\ntemperatures into the 80s, although cooler temperatures will be\ncommon along the coast due to the onshore flow. Saturday night\nwill be on the cool side for late July with lows into the\n40s/50s. \n\nDry conditions will persist on Sunday with partly to mostly\nsunny skies and near average high temperatures into the lower\n80s. Low temperatures on Sunday night will mainly be into the\n50s with partly cloudy skies. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nEarly next week, an omega block pattern establishes across the \nregion as the western ridge amplifies and Atlantic high builds \nnorthward, parking a longwave upper level trough over the East Coast \nthrough at least late week. An upper-level low over Hudson Bay will \ndeepen the trough, driving a surface low and accompanying cold front \nacross New England, with a focus in the Tue-Wed timeframe. Model \nguidance shows notable spread regarding the surface low. The \nGFS/GEFS favors a weaker, more progressive solution further south, \nwhereas the ECMWF/EPS depicts a stronger (wetter), closed low \nfurther north. \n\nThe predominant forecast impact will be increasing shower activity \nand thunderstorms from late Monday through mid-week, potentially \nlingering into Thursday if the trough slows. Activity will be \nenhanced diurnally rather than a continuous washout. Southerly \nreturn flow will advect deeper moisture across the region, elevating \nPWATs into the 1.50\u20132.00\" range alongside dew points in the 60s. \nThis juicy moisture profile, combined with lowering heights aloft \nand steepening mid-level lapse rates, will support modest \ninstability (mainly in the afternoons) capable of supporting \nlocalized brief downpours. While initial guidance kept the core jet \ndynamics displaced to our south, there will be shear and CAPE \noverlap ahead of the front supporting storms. Limiting factors \ninclude cloud cover, height falls, and falling 1000\u2013500 mb thickness \nvalues, with surface temperatures seasonably cool in the 70s before \nweak ridging attempts to push the trough offshore by next weekend.",
